4.1  Hard drives, SSDs and NVMe

TL;DR
  - A hard drive (HDD) reads magnetic platters with a moving head: fine at long
    sequential reads, slow at jumping around (a few milliseconds per seek, about
    a hundred random reads per second).
  - An SSD stores data in flash chips, with nothing moving: random reads take
    tens of microseconds, so it's thousands of times better at them.
  - SATA SSDs are capped by their interface at about 550 MB/s. NVMe SSDs talk to
    the CPU over PCIe: several GB/s, and many requests in parallel.

      lsblk -d -o NAME,ROTA,TRAN,MODEL,SIZE     # ROTA 1: spinning

On your machine
  nvme0n1   Samsung SSD 980 PRO 500GB: NVMe flash, 476.9 GiB · scheduler none ·
            8 hardware queues · volatile write cache · TRIM
  sda       USB DISK 3.0: USB drive, 30.0 GiB · scheduler mq-deadline · no
            volatile write cache

Pitfalls
  - Virtual machines often report their disks as rotational (ROTA 1), even when
    the host stores them on an SSD.
  - An NVMe SSD in an M.2 slot wired for SATA only, or a PCIe slot with fewer
    lanes, runs well below its rated speed: check the board's manual.
  - USB sticks and SD cards are flash too, but with simple controllers: slow at
    small writes, and they wear out much sooner.
  - Speeds on the box are for sequential transfers at their best. Small random
    writes are a fraction of that (4.4).

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 4.1 --why

